  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nHigh pressure continues to build off to the east this afternoon and \nevening as a low pressure system moves through Northern Ontario. As \nthe low moves to the east, the corresponding warm front has moved \nnorth through the region this afternoon with the cold front passing \nthrough late tonight into Wednesday morning. Precipitation chances \nwill start to increase with the frontal passage in Northwestern Ohio \nand spread across the lake shore to the east. More towards Central \nOhio will remain fairly dry for now as an upper level ridge sits \nover the southeast CONUS and the shortwaves supporting precipitation \nwill move along the northern periphery. As for the precipitation \nchances overnight tonight, there will be a marginal chance for \nsevere weather across Northwestern Ohio, but given the time frame \nthe front will be moving through, instability will be limited. There \nwill be around 500 J/kg of MLCAPE, around 30 knots of bulk shear, \nand DCAPE will be limited as well at roughly 600-800. So overall, \nthe severe weather chances will be on the lower end, but not zero if \na thunderstorm can strengthen enough to have stronger wind gusts. \nWith that, SPC has placed the majority of Northwestern Ohio in a \nMarginal risk for severe thunderstorms with the main threat being \ndamaging winds. Severe threat should dwindle into the morning hours \ntomorrow. Additionally, there will be a low chance of a heavy rain \nthreat for Northwestern Ohio with these thunderstorms as PWAT values \nwill climb to around 2 inches in the area. With instability and \nlapse rates lacking though, the risk will be low, but not zero for \nany thunderstorm to drop a quick 1-2 inches of rain. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nThe cold front will continue to push southward Wednesday morning \nthough will be fairly weak. There will be little air mass change \nexpected with it and should stall out across the Ohio Valley \nWednesday evening. Not expecting any thunderstorms to be severe \nduring the day Wednesday as lapse rates will be lacking across the \nregion. There may be a few storms that generate some strong gusts \nthough as instability will be higher with daytime heating and dew \npoints will climb into the upper 60s to low 70s. Rounds of \nprecipitation is expected through Thursday evening. Currently there \nis some variance in model guidance as to where the front sets up to \nthe south and where the shortwaves are that will support \nthunderstorms. There will also continue to be a heavy rain threat \nthrough Thursday as PWAT values will still be around 2 inches, but \nwith lapse rates on the lower end, the heavy rain threat will be on \nthe lower end. That being said, with more instability across the \nregion tomorrow and Thursday, there will be a little more help \ngetting some thunderstorms to produce heavy rain. By Thursday \nevening into Friday morning, the frontal boundary will push further \nsouth as high pressure builds in from the north across the Great \nLakes and precipitation chances will decrease to end the week.",
